Make an answered NFS COMMIT mean the data is on the disk.

The macOS client writes UNSTABLE and then sends COMMIT, and takes the reply
as its data being durable. nfsd answers a COMMIT by calling vfs_fsync_range()
over the client's range and nothing else. On ntfs3 that is
generic_file_fsync, which writes the file's pages in that range and its
inode, and not the volume's own metadata, which is what syncfs(2) writes.

Measured on a real drive on 2026-09-10: after a 32 MiB write and fsync from
the Mac, 464 kB was still dirty in the guest. An fsync of that one file
inside the guest cleared 208 kB of it, and syncfs of the volume cleared the
other 256 kB. The Mac had been told all of it was stable.

On an export that asks for sync writes, which is the only case where nfsd
syncs anything, COMMIT now fsyncs the whole file rather than the client's
range, and when that succeeds syncs the file's filesystem exactly as
syncfs(2) does: sync_filesystem() with s_umount held for reading. An error
from either goes through the switch that was already there, so a failed sync
still resets the write verifier and the client sends its writes again. The
code that clamped the client's range to s_maxbytes is removed, having
nothing left to feed.

What it costs is a sync of the whole volume on every COMMIT. That has not
been measured yet.

